"""Focused worked operations for previously underexplained TOC/compiler topics."""
from . import T

T('5-cfg-pda','cfg-derivation','Context-free grammars','Build a leftmost derivation and its parse tree',r'''
A context-free grammar consists of terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, a start nonterminal and productions with one nonterminal on the left. A derivation begins at the start symbol and repeatedly replaces a nonterminal by a production's right-hand side. Only strings containing terminals alone belong to the generated language. A leftmost derivation always expands the leftmost remaining nonterminal; this fixes the expansion order without changing the grammar's language.
The balanced-parenthesis grammar S→(S)S | ε separates a matched outer pair from a following balanced suffix. The first S inside the parentheses describes nesting, while the second S describes concatenation. Each nonempty expansion adds a matched pair; an epsilon expansion closes an unfinished recursive obligation. Record the entire sentential form after each rewrite, including all remaining nonterminals. A parse tree stores the same production choices hierarchically. Reading its terminal leaves left to right must reproduce the target string, and every internal node must use an actual grammar production.
''','Using S→(S)S | ε, give a leftmost derivation of (()) and explain the tree.',r"""Open the outer pair::S\Rightarrow(S)S::The target begins with a matched outer pair; the final S will describe any suffix after it.~Nest another pair::(S)S\Rightarrow((S)S)S::Expand the leftmost S, which lies inside the outer pair.~Close the inner content::((S)S)S\Rightarrow(()S)S::Use epsilon on the leftmost S because the inner pair contains no further parentheses.~Close both suffixes::(()S)S\Rightarrow(())S\Rightarrow(())::Both remaining S symbols derive epsilon, in leftmost order.""", 'The leftmost derivation produces (()); its tree has one pair nested inside another and empty following suffixes.','Reading the terminal leaves gives exactly (()). Every prefix has at least as many opening as closing parentheses, and the final counts are equal.')

T('5-cfg-pda','cfl-union-construction','Context-free languages','Establish a context-free language by constructing a grammar',r'''
A language is context free when some context-free grammar generates exactly its strings. A positive classification therefore needs a construction with both soundness and completeness: every generated string has the desired property, and every desired string can be generated. A grammar's recursive appearance alone does not establish that its language is nonregular, because regular languages are also context free.
Context-free languages are closed under union. To combine two grammars, first rename their nonterminals so they are disjoint, then introduce a fresh start symbol choosing one old start or the other. The choice is made for the whole derivation; it does not freely mix the component languages' terminal strings. For equal-count blocks, a production A→aAb adds one leading a and one trailing b together. Repeating it n times and then using epsilon gives every aⁿbⁿ and nothing else. This invariant supplies a proof for all lengths, beyond checking a few examples. The same construction can use c in place of b for another component.
''','Construct a CFG for {aⁿbⁿ:n≥0} ∪ {aⁿcⁿ:n≥0}. Test aacc and aabc.',r"""Build separate components::A\to aAb\mid\varepsilon,\quad B\to aBc\mid\varepsilon::A generates matching a/b blocks; B generates matching a/c blocks.~Choose one language::S\to A\mid B::A new start symbol makes a whole-string union without mixing the two grammars.~Derive a member::S\Rightarrow B\Rightarrow aBc\Rightarrow aaBcc\Rightarrow aacc::Choose the c component and apply its recursive production twice.~Rule out the mixed word::::A terminal derivation through A contains no c, while one through B contains no b. The word aabc contains both and cannot be derived.""", 'The displayed grammar proves the union context free. It accepts aacc and rejects aabc.','After n recursive expansions, A yields aⁿAbⁿ and B yields aⁿBcⁿ. Replacing the last nonterminal by epsilon proves exact counts and covers every member, including the empty word.')

T('5-decidability','tm-tape-trace','Turing machines','Trace a Turing machine one transition at a time',r'''
A Turing machine has a finite control state, a read/write tape, a head position and a transition function. A transition uses the current state and scanned symbol to choose a new state, the symbol written at that cell, and a head movement. A complete configuration records the tape, head and state. Changing only the state in a trace omits the memory operation that distinguishes this model from finite automata.
Use a two-way infinite tape with integer-indexed cells and blank symbols outside the finite input. The input alphabet here is just {1}, so every input is a unary number: n ones represent n. A unary incrementer scans right across the original ones, replaces the first blank by a one, moves left and enters a halting state. The state called q_h has no outgoing transitions. Tape blanks are part of the tape alphabet, not extra symbols in the input. Counting transitions establishes termination: n scanning steps and one writing step halt on every valid input, including the empty input representing zero.
''','Trace input 111 with the head at cell 0. Rules: δ(q_s,1)=(q_s,1,R) and δ(q_s,blank)=(q_h,1,L).',r"""Read cell zero::\delta(q_s,1)=(q_s,1,R)::Write the same 1, retain q_s and move the head from cell 0 to cell 1; the tape is still 111 followed by blanks.~Read the remaining ones::(q_s,1)\longrightarrow(q_s,2)\longrightarrow(q_s,3)::These pairs record state and head index. Two more scanning transitions preserve cells 1 and 2 and reach the blank at cell 3.~Write the increment::\delta(q_s,\square)=(q_h,1,L)::Replace cell 3's blank with 1, enter q_h and move the head to cell 2.~Inspect the halted configuration::::The tape now contains 1111 in cells 0 through 3; the head scans cell 2 and no further rule applies in q_h.""", 'The machine halts after four transitions with output 1111 and its head at cell 2.','Three input ones caused three rightward scanning moves, and exactly one previously blank cell became 1. For n input ones the output has n+1 ones and the run takes n+1 transitions.')

T('5-decidability','halting-diagonal','Halting problem','Prove the halting problem undecidable by diagonalization',r'''
The halting problem asks whether a supplied machine eventually halts on a supplied input, whether by accepting or rejecting. A proposed decider must answer correctly and halt for every encoded machine/input pair. Simulating a machine can confirm halting when it happens, but no arbitrary finite observation establishes that a machine will run forever.
Diagonalization assumes such a total decider H exists and then builds another machine D that deliberately contradicts H on self-input. On a valid machine encoding x, D asks whether the encoded machine halts when given that same encoding x. If H predicts halting, D loops forever; if H predicts nonhalting, D halts. Invalid encodings may be handled by halting immediately, so they cause no issue. D is a finite machine description because its code just calls the assumed H and branches on its answer. Running D on its own valid encoding forces the prediction to disagree with the actual behavior in either case. The contradiction rules out H, rather than prohibiting solutions for particular machines or bounded simulation questions.
''','Assume a total correct halting decider H(M,w). Construct D and analyze H(D,〈D〉).',r"""Define the opposite behavior::::On input x, D calls H on the machine encoded by x with input x. A yes answer makes D loop; a no answer makes D halt.~Supply its own encoding::D(\langle D\rangle)::The encoding is valid and refers to the very machine whose branch behavior was just defined.~Examine a yes answer::::If H predicts that D on its own encoding halts, D follows the yes branch and loops forever, making the prediction false.~Examine a no answer::::If H predicts that this run never halts, D follows the no branch and halts, again making the prediction false.""", 'Both possible answers contradict correctness, so no total halting decider exists.','The same run is queried in H and executed by D: machine D with input 〈D〉. The argument does not confuse accepting with halting or assume the ability to obtain a nonhalting answer by simulation.')

T('5-decidability','undecidable-emptiness','Undecidability','Show that machine-language emptiness is undecidable',r'''
A decision problem is undecidable when no algorithm halts with the correct yes/no answer on every allowed input. This is a statement about a universal solver, not about whether selected instances have easily proved answers. To prove a new problem undecidable, transform instances of an already undecidable problem into its instances and show how an assumed solver would settle the original problem.
Consider whether a Turing machine's recognized language is empty. Given a machine M and input w, construct a new machine N that ignores its own input y and simulates M on w. If that simulation ever halts, whether accepting or rejecting, N accepts y. If it never halts, N never accepts y. Thus N recognizes every string or no strings according to the original run's halting behavior. Constructing N's finite description just embeds M and w in simulation code; it does not run the simulation to completion. A decider for emptiness would therefore decide halting after reversing its answer. This separates the termination of the reduction algorithm from the possibly infinite execution of the constructed machine.
''','Use the known undecidability of HALT to prove that deciding L(N)=∅ is impossible.',r"""Construct the target machine::::From (M,w), output code for N: ignore y, simulate M(w), and accept y if the simulation halts. This code construction always terminates.~Analyze a halting source::M(w)\text{ halts}\Rightarrow L(N)=\Sigma^*::Every y triggers the same eventually halting simulation, after which N accepts it.~Analyze a looping source::M(w)\text{ does not halt}\Rightarrow L(N)=\varnothing::No run of N reaches its accept action, so its recognized language is empty.~Use the assumed emptiness solver::::If a total solver says empty, answer that M(w) does not halt; if it says nonempty, answer that M(w) halts. This would decide the known undecidable problem.""", 'Machine-language emptiness is undecidable.','A rejecting halt of M must still make N accept, because the source question asks about halting. Inspecting that case ensures the transform preserves the intended yes/no relation.')

T('5-decidability','reduction-machine-construction','Reductions','Construct and verify a mapping reduction',r'''
A mapping reduction A≤ₘB is an always-halting computable transformation f satisfying x∈A exactly when f(x)∈B. To prove B undecidable from a known undecidable A, compute f and then invoke a hypothetical B decider. The transform must produce an instance without first answering A. A machine constructed by the transform may run forever; the algorithm writing that machine's finite description must not.
For A_TM, the source question is whether M accepts w. For HALT, the target question is whether a machine halts on its given input. These differ when M halts rejecting. Build a machine N that ignores its own input, simulates M on the embedded word w, and halts only when that simulation accepts. On simulated rejection N deliberately enters an infinite loop; on simulated nontermination it keeps simulating. Checking all three behaviors is necessary: a wrapper that simply stops whenever M stops would reduce a halting question, but would not preserve acceptance. State both the produced machine and the fixed input supplied to it in the target instance.
''','Assume A_TM is known undecidable. Map (M,w) to a HALT instance with matching yes answers.',r"""Write a finite wrapper::f(\langle M,w\rangle)=\langle N,\varepsilon\rangle::N ignores its input, simulates the embedded M(w), halts on acceptance and deliberately loops on rejection.~Check source acceptance::M\text{ accepts }w\Rightarrow N(\varepsilon)\text{ halts}::The simulation reaches acceptance and activates the wrapper's halting branch.~Check both negative behaviors::::If M rejects w, N loops after observing rejection. If M itself loops, N continues simulating forever. In either case N does not halt.~Conclude the reduction::\langle M,w\rangle\in A_{TM}\iff f(\langle M,w\rangle)\in HALT::A HALT decider composed with this terminating code transform would decide A_TM, so HALT is undecidable.""", 'The wrapper establishes A_TM≤ₘHALT; both source no cases map to target no cases.','No step in constructing N needs to know whether M accepts. The deliberate rejection loop is essential, because rejecting is a source no answer despite being a halting computation.')

T('6-lexical','token-records','Tokens','Convert lexemes into token kinds and attributes',r'''
A token occurrence is the lexer output for one recognized source fragment. Its kind is a category such as identifier, number, assignment or punctuation. The lexeme is the actual sequence of source characters. An optional attribute carries information needed later: an identifier can reference a symbol-table entry and a numeric token can carry its numeric value. These are related pieces of information, not interchangeable names for one object.
Token counting concerns occurrences in the input, even when their kinds or lexemes repeat. Two appearances of the same identifier still produce two token occurrences, although both can point to the same declaration entry. When whitespace is ignored it separates neighboring lexemes but emits no token. The parser usually reads token kinds to recognize grammatical structure, and semantic analysis or code generation consults attributes. To work a token-record problem, first mark the source boundaries under the specified lexical rules, then label each fragment's kind, then attach only the attributes that the question's model defines. Preserve punctuation as tokens unless its rule explicitly discards it.
''','Tokenize sum = sum + 12; ignoring whitespace. Let both occurrences of sum refer to symbol-table entry p_sum, and store integer values on NUMBER tokens.',r"""Mark lexeme boundaries::::The fragments are sum, =, sum, +, 12 and ;. The spaces emit no tokens, and the two digits form one number.~Attach identifier information::\langle\mathrm{ID},p_{\mathrm{sum}}\rangle::Emit this record twice, once for each sum occurrence. A shared symbol-table reference does not merge the two occurrences.~Attach the remaining kinds::\langle\mathrm{ASSIGN}\rangle,\ \langle\mathrm{PLUS}\rangle,\ \langle\mathrm{NUMBER},12\rangle,\ \langle\mathrm{SEMI}\rangle::The number attribute stores 12; the operators and delimiter need no additional attribute in this model.~Count occurrences::::The ordered stream is ID, ASSIGN, ID, PLUS, NUMBER, SEMI: six occurrences with five distinct kinds.""", 'There are six token occurrences. Both ID tokens have attribute p_sum, while NUMBER has attribute 12.','Concatenating the six lexemes reconstructs the input after removing whitespace. The repeated identifier accounts for why occurrence count six differs from distinct-kind count five.')

T('6-lexical','regex-token-rule','Regular expressions','Use a regular expression as a whole-lexeme token rule',r'''
A classical regular expression describes a language using union, concatenation and Kleene star. In lexical analysis, this language gives the valid lexemes of a token class. A token candidate must match the whole lexeme, not merely contain a substring that matches the expression. The lexer also needs a policy for choosing among matching token rules, but that policy is separate from the language defined by one expression.
An identifier rule can require a lowercase letter first and then permit any sequence of lowercase letters or digits. Concatenation makes the initial letter mandatory. The starred suffix may have length zero, so a single letter is valid. Digits become legal only after the first character; beginning with a digit violates this rule even if later characters are letters. Such bounded position conditions are regular and can be implemented by a DFA with a start state, an accepting identifier state and a rejecting sink. When testing a candidate, decompose it according to the expression and check every character against its allowed position. Do not introduce programming-library features such as backreferences into the mathematical definition.
''','With letter={a,…,z} and digit={0,…,9}, write the identifier regex and classify x, x7a, 7x and the empty string.',r"""Build the expression::\mathrm{letter}(\mathrm{letter}\mid\mathrm{digit})^*::The first factor supplies one compulsory letter; every later symbol belongs to either allowed class.~Test a one-character identifier::x=x\cdot\varepsilon::The first factor matches x and the starred suffix contributes zero symbols, so x is valid.~Test a mixed suffix::x7a=x\cdot7a::The initial x is a letter and the suffix is two repetitions, digit 7 then letter a, so the entire lexeme matches.~Reject invalid starts::::The candidate 7x fails the compulsory first-letter factor. The empty string also fails because only the suffix, not the first factor, can be empty.""", 'The rule is letter(letter|digit)*. It accepts x and x7a, and rejects 7x and the empty string.','A three-state DFA can move from start to an accepting identifier state on a letter, loop there on letters/digits, and send other cases to a sink; this agrees with all four classifications.')

T('6-parsing','parse-tree-trace','Parsing','Recover grammar structure from a token sequence',r'''
Parsing determines whether an ordered token sequence can be generated by a grammar and, when it can, recovers a derivation or parse tree. Lexical validity alone is insufficient: every token may be individually valid while their sequence violates the grammar. A successful parse accounts for the whole input, so a valid prefix followed by leftover tokens is not enough.
In a top-down view, select a production for the start symbol and recursively parse the portions assigned to its nonterminals. In a bottom-up view, reduce suitable right-hand sides to nonterminals until the start symbol remains. Either method must respect the same grammatical structure. The grammar S→aSb | ab generates one or more a symbols followed by exactly as many b symbols. Its recursive production adds one outer a and b around a smaller instance; its base case consumes ab. The productions share an initial a, so this grammar is not an LL(1) grammar as written, and an unrestricted top-down explanation should not be mistaken for a conflict-free one-lookahead table. Check the resulting tree's leaves as well as its production labels.
''','Parse aabb with S→aSb | ab. Then explain why abab fails under this grammar.',r"""Choose the root production::::The four-symbol input is too long for S→ab alone, so its root uses S→aSb. Match the initial a and final b, assigning the middle ab to S.~Parse the child::S\Rightarrow aSb\Rightarrow aabb::The child uses the base production S→ab; every nonterminal has now been expanded.~Read the tree leaves::::The root's leaves are a, then its child's a and b, then the root's closing b. They consume all four input tokens in order.~Test the competing word::::If abab used aSb at the root, its middle would be ba, which S cannot generate; if it used ab, two tokens would remain. Both root choices fail.""", 'aabb has a valid tree with one recursive node and one base node. abab is rejected.','The grammar invariant is aⁿbⁿ for n≥1. aabb has n=2, while abab switches back to a after entering the b block and violates that invariant.')

T('6-parsing','lr-complete-run','LR','Construct LR states and execute shifts and reductions',r'''
An LR parser reads input from left to right and constructs a rightmost derivation in reverse. An item A→α·β records the recognized prefix α of a production. Closure repeatedly adds every production of a nonterminal immediately after a dot. The goto operation moves dots across a specified grammar symbol and then takes closure; the resulting item sets are parser states.
Augment the grammar with a fresh start production S′→S. A terminal transition creates a shift action, a completed non-start item creates a reduction, and the completed augmented item permits acceptance on the end marker. For LR(0), a reduction applies on every terminal lookahead; conflicting required actions would show that the grammar is not LR(0). The state stack begins with state 0. A shift consumes one token and pushes its destination state. A reduction by A→β consumes no input, pops as many states as there are symbols in β, and pushes goto of the newly exposed state on A. Recording both the state stack and remaining input prevents confusing a reduction with token consumption.
''','For S′→S and S→aS | b, construct the relevant LR(0) states and parse ab$. Number goto(I0,a)=I1, goto(I0,b)=I2 and goto(I0,S)=I3.',r"""Close the start state::I_0=\{S'\to\cdot S,\ S\to\cdot aS,\ S\to\cdot b\}::The dot before S in the augmented item adds both S productions.~Advance and close::I_1=\{S\to a\cdot S,\ S\to\cdot aS,\ S\to\cdot b\}::Goto on a advances the item and closure again adds both S productions. Goto(I1,a)=I1 and goto(I1,b)=I2.~Identify completed states::I_2=\{S\to b\cdot\},\ I_3=\{S'\to S\cdot\},\ I_4=\{S\to aS\cdot\}::I4 is goto(I1,S). I2 reduces S→b and I4 reduces S→aS on every terminal; I3 accepts only on the end marker.~Read table actions::::ACTION[0,a]=shift 1; ACTION[1,b]=shift 2; ACTION[2,$]=reduce S→b; ACTION[4,$]=reduce S→aS; ACTION[3,$]=accept. GOTO[0,S]=3 and GOTO[1,S]=4.~Shift the input::[0]\longrightarrow[0,1]\longrightarrow[0,1,2]::Consume a and then b. The grammar-symbol stack is ab and the remaining lookahead is $.~Reduce the base::[0,1,2]\longrightarrow[0,1,4]::Reduce S→b, pop one state, and push GOTO[1,S]=4. The symbol stack becomes aS and the lookahead stays $.~Reduce and accept::[0,1,4]\longrightarrow[0,3]::Reduce S→aS, pop two states, then push GOTO[0,S]=3. The stack now represents S and ACTION[3,$] accepts.""", 'ab is accepted after two shifts, two reductions and one accept action.','The reversed reductions reconstruct S⇒aS⇒ab. Every reduction state contains only one completed non-start item and no shift item, so these LR(0) actions create no conflicts.')

T('6-ir-dataflow','local-optimisation-block','Local optimisation','Optimize a basic block without reusing stale values',r'''
Local optimisation transforms instructions inside one basic block: a straight-line region with one entry and no internal branching. A valid transformation preserves observable behavior under the specified language rules. Typical operations include constant folding, copy propagation, common-subexpression elimination and removal of dead assignments. A result is dead only when it has no subsequent use and no required value at block exit; removing its computation must also preserve side effects.
A previously computed expression can be reused while its operands retain their values. Assigning to an operand invalidates that availability even when the expression's spelling stays the same. A copy assignment can often be removed by replacing its later uses with the original temporary, provided neither value changes along the way. For this example, all variables are ordinary nonaliased mathematical integers; addition has no overflow or hidden side effects. Only the printed values are observable, and no variable is live at block exit. State these assumptions before deleting instructions. Verifying one sample input is a helpful check, but the correctness argument must explain why both printed expressions retain their values for every possible input.
''','Inputs a,b are set before the block. Optimize: t1=a+b; t2=a+b; unused=9; a=a+1; t3=a+b; print(t2,t3). Only print is observable and no variables are live after the block.',r"""Reuse the first expression::::Before either operand changes, the second a+b equals t1. Replace t2=a+b by t2=t1.~Propagate the copy::::The temporary t1 is never reassigned, so replace the later print use of t2 by t1 and remove the copy assignment.~Remove the dead assignment::::unused=9 has no later use, no live-out demand and no side effect. Delete it.~Respect the operand change::::Keep a=a+1 and the later t3=a+b. Changing a killed availability of the original a+b, so replacing t3 by t1 would be incorrect.~Check concrete values::a=2,\ b=3\Rightarrow(t1,t3)=(5,6)::Both the original and transformed blocks print 5 followed by 6; stale reuse would incorrectly print 5 twice.""", 'Optimized block: t1=a+b; a=a+1; t3=a+b; print(t1,t3).','For arbitrary initial a₀,b₀ the two outputs are a₀+b₀ and a₀+1+b₀ in both blocks. This equality, together with preserving the print effect, verifies the transformation for every input.')

T('6-ir-dataflow','liveness-cfg-fixed-point','Liveness analysis','Iterate live-variable equations across a loop',r'''
A variable is live at a program point when some continuation reads its current value before a new assignment overwrites it. Liveness is a backward may-analysis: demand on any successor path is enough. For each basic block, USE contains variables read before their first definition in that block, and DEF contains variables assigned there. A variable can occur in both sets if its old value is read before being overwritten.
Compute OUT[B] as the union of successors' IN sets, then IN[B]=USE[B]∪(OUT[B]−DEF[B]). Exit blocks with no successors have empty OUT when there is no extra live-out boundary requirement. Initialize all sets empty and repeatedly apply the equations. Loops can carry demand back through earlier blocks, so one reverse scan or one formula evaluation does not establish the answer. In the synchronous iteration below, each round reads the previous round's IN sets. The sets only gain variables and the variable universe is finite, so iteration terminates at the least fixed point. Verify that both IN and OUT stop changing; an unchanged IN round alone can hide an OUT update.
''','CFG: B1: x=1; goto B2. B2: y=x+1; if p goto B3 else B4. B3: x=y; goto B2. B4: return y. Find all live-in/live-out sets by synchronous iteration from empty sets.',r"""Extract block facts::::USE/DEF pairs are B1: ∅/{x}; B2: {x,p}/{y}; B3: {y}/{x}; B4: {y}/∅. Successors are B1→B2, B2→{B3,B4}, B3→B2, and B4 has none.~Apply round one::(IN_1,IN_2,IN_3,IN_4)=(\varnothing,\{x,p\},\{y\},\{y\})::All previous successor IN sets were empty, so round-one OUT sets are all empty and IN is each block's USE.~Propagate round two::(OUT_1,OUT_2,OUT_3,OUT_4)=(\{x,p\},\{y\},\{x,p\},\varnothing)::Union round-one successor demand. B1 kills old x and gains IN={p}; B3 kills old x but retains p and uses y, giving IN={y,p}. B2 and B4 keep their round-one IN sets.~Carry loop demand to the branch::OUT_2=IN_3\cup IN_4=\{y,p\}::Round three adds p to B2's OUT. Its IN remains {x,p}, since y is defined in B2 and x,p are read there. The other OUT sets retain their round-two values.~Verify the full fixed point::IN_1=\{p\},\quad IN_2=\{x,p\},\quad IN_3=\{y,p\},\quad IN_4=\{y\}::A fourth application reproduces all these IN sets and all round-three OUT sets, so both halves of the equations are stable.""", 'Final IN/OUT pairs: B1 {p}/{x,p}; B2 {x,p}/{y,p}; B3 {y,p}/{x,p}; B4 {y}/∅.','p remains live through the loop because B2 can test it again. Old x is not live into B1 or B3, since each overwrites x before using that old value. Substituting every final pair into both equations reproduces it.')
